      "title": "The Dimension of a Span",
      "uuid": "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186121058,
      "id": "df182889-cfbe-5906-abbc-45fc241177c2",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "3519",
      "stepId": "17592186120736",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186119661,
      "id": "fd1734a2-5193-5182-9a6f-4a024ee32ce5",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186119847,
        "title": "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns"
      },
      "markdown": "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-5415",
      "stepId": "17592186120737",
      "title": "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns",
      "workedSolution": "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186121068,
      "id": "1383e230-aa34-5f36-a91f-c60846be8cc4",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Note that the number of pivot columns always coincides with the number of non-zero rows in the corresponding row echelon form matrix.\nSo, technically, \n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *pivot columns* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.\n\nOr, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *non-zero rows* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "4151",
      "stepId": "17592186120761",
      "title": "An Alternative Definition of the Rank"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186119662,
      "id": "8cc42d44-bd5b-5eb5-9e55-ef7a7d93c31a",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186119848,
        "title": "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"
      },
      "markdown": "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-5417",
      "stepId": "17592186120738",
      "title": "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection",
      "workedSolution": "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186119663,
      "id": "c27d4e1c-92bf-502f-9c02-c0bf68357dbf",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186119849,
        "title": "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"
      },
      "markdown": "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-5418",
      "stepId": "17592186120739",
      "title": "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction",
      "workedSolution": "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"
    }
  ],
